\chapter{Scheduling and Execution Dispatch}
\label{ch:scheduling}

Operating system schedulers in modern high-performance environments face
a multi-dimensional challenge: balancing sub-millisecond latency
guarantees for real-time task continuations with strict fair-share
resource allocation across throughput-bound workloads.

Telix addresses this by decoupling user-level execution contexts from
physical hardware dispatch using an \textbf{M:N continuation executor model},
enforcing \textbf{Hierarchical Fair-Share allocation} via an \textbf{Earliest
Eligible Virtual Deadline First (EEVDF)} engine
\footcite{stoica96eevdf, zijlstra23eevdf}, and coordinating
cross-partition parallelism through \textbf{gang and symbiotic coscheduling}
\footcite{ousterhout82gang}.

\section{Two Execution Domains}
\label{sec:two_domains}

Telix separates execution into two fundamentally different domains.
This distinction is essential to understanding the relationship between
the M:N continuation model and the EEVDF scheduler.

\begin{description}
  \item[\textbf{TCB-Internal Continuations} (framekernel code).]
    Interrupt handlers, timer callbacks, page-fault dispatch,
    shootdown IPI processing, and IOMMU invalidation — all code
    within the framekernel's Trusted Computing Base — execute as
    non-preemptible, run-to-completion continuations.  Like Linux
    softirqs or timer callbacks, these continuations never block
    and never yield mid-execution: they service a bounded event and
    return.  Because the TCB is deliberately small, no individual
    TCB continuation can hold a CPU for an unbounded duration.
    These continuations are \emph{not} scheduled by EEVDF; they are
    dispatched directly from the interrupt vector table or from the
    completion of a prior TCB continuation.

  \item[\textbf{User Futures} (the $M$ in $M\!:\!N$).]
    User-space tasks — application threads, personality-server
    request handlers, external pager logic, filesystem operations —
    are implemented as stackless Rust \texttt{Future} state
    machines.  These \emph{are} scheduled by the EEVDF engine.
    They may yield at any \texttt{await} point; the EEVDF executor
    tracks their virtual time, computes eligibility and virtual
    deadlines, and selects which future to poll next.

    A stackless future has no per-task stack, so it cannot be
    suspended mid-poll by a timer interrupt: the executor runs a
    future until it hits an \texttt{await} and voluntarily yields,
    then consults the EEVDF red-black tree before re-polling.  If
    the just-yielded future has exceeded its fair share (negative
    lag, $L_i < 0$), the executor polls a different future instead.
    This cooperative fast path is zero-cost and suffices for
    I/O-bound work, which dominates.

    The absence of a stack bars \emph{timer} preemption only.  A
    trap (syscall or page fault) is a different, involuntary kind
    of suspension point: the future is not \emph{rebuilt} but
    \emph{parked}, its register state captured in a transient trap
    frame (not a runtime stack) while its persistent state remains
    in the struct.  The trap is surfaced to the executor as a
    synthetic \texttt{Pending} plus a blocked event, and the future
    resumes later by restoring the trap frame and re-entering the
    faulting instruction.  This is the streamlined, Nemesis/K42-style
    upcall of \S\ref{sec:async_page_faults}: the stackless model and the
    blocking-syscall/page-fault case compose because a trap frame is
    transient machine context, not per-task stack storage.

    Preemption of user work is provided by two \emph{stack
    carriers}, described in \S\ref{sec:preemption}: (A) a kernel
    stack at syscall/fault entry, making the kernel-side execution
    of a future preemptible; and (B) a demotion path that migrates
    a compute-bound future to a preemptible carrier thread with a
    real stack.  Both preserve the stackless, serialisable nature
    of user futures in the common case.
\end{description}

