\section{Execution Model: M:N Threading via Async Continuations}
\label{sec:mn_threading}

Traditional kernel thread models rely on 1:1 mapping, where every
user-space thread maps directly to a kernel task with its own
preallocated kernel stack (typically 8--16~KiB). In high-concurrency
environments with tens of thousands of tasks, 1:1 models cause severe
memory footprint bloat and frequent hardware context-switch overhead.

Conversely, legacy user-level M:N threading models (mapping $M$ user
threads onto $N$ kernel OS threads) suffered from severe
kernel-blindness: if one user thread triggered a blocking system call or
page fault, the underlying kernel thread blocked, stalling the remaining
$M-1$ user threads bound to that executor \footcite{ande92}.

\subsection{The Telix M:N Continuation Model}
Telix's M:N executor combines three well-understood, published
mechanisms — io\_uring-style async submission rings, memory-mapped
completion queues, and EEVDF per-core scheduling — into a coherent
two-execution-domain architecture.  None of these mechanisms is novel;
the contribution is their integration into a unified framekernel design
alongside the virtual-memory innovations that are the system's primary
research contribution.  The three classic scheduler-activation failure
modes identified by Anderson et al.~\footcite{ande91,ande92} are avoided
by design, as detailed in \S\ref{sec:anderson_failure_modes} below.

The components:

\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{User-Space Tasks ($M$):}] Light-weight user tasks are implemented as stackless Rust \texttt{Future} state machines. Tasks allocate zero dedicated kernel stack memory while idle.

    \item[\textbf{Kernel Executors ($N$):}] A fixed pool of $N$ kernel worker continuations (typically one per physical CPU core of the Framekernel domain) poll and drive eligible user tasks.  Each executor divides cleanly into two parts.  Its \emph{management loop} — choosing the eligible future, updating lag, and deciding what to poll next — is core-adjacent, non-preemptible TCB code, and it is \emph{all} the core does; when no user future is eligible, the executor idles (e.g., via \texttt{WFI}) and resumes on the next interrupt.  The \texttt{Future::poll()} invocation itself is a \emph{handoff} out of the TCB into the preemptible layer: it executes arbitrary user code and, under Carrier~A, arbitrary preemptible kernel code, neither of which is a TCB continuation.

    \item[\textbf{Non-Blocking I/O Boundary:}] System calls and hardware I/O operations are submitted asynchronously via memory-mapped submission rings (\texttt{io\_uring}-style). Rather than blocking the kernel executor thread, a waiting task yields its state machine back to the scheduler ring, allowing executor $N$ to immediately poll the next ready task.
\end{description}

\subsection{Anderson Failure Modes and How Telix Avoids Them}
\label{sec:anderson_failure_modes}

Scheduler activations were abandoned in practice because user-level M:N
models without kernel cooperation suffer three well-characterised
failure modes~\footcite{ande91,ande92}.  Telix does not claim to have
\emph{solved} scheduler activations; rather, the three Anderson modes
are avoided by construction because each maps to a published,
independently-validated mechanism:

\begin{description}
  \item[\textbf{Mode~1 — blocking-syscall stall.}]
    If one user thread issues a blocking syscall, the kernel thread
    executing it blocks, and the $M-1$ other user threads sharing that
    kernel thread stall.  Telix avoids this entirely: all I/O is
    submitted through memory-mapped submission rings
    (\texttt{io\_uring}-style, a concept originating in FlexSC~\footcite{soar10,axbo19}), a future awaiting
    I/O returns \texttt{Poll::Pending} without the kernel executor ever
    entering a blocking path, and the executor immediately polls the
    next eligible future.  The kernel executor never blocks on a
    syscall, so there is no carrier thread to stall.

  \item[\textbf{Mode~2 — lost wakeups.}]
    In a pure user-level scheduler, the kernel does not know the
    scheduler's state.  A wakeup (I/O completion, timer fire) that
    arrives while the scheduler is between poll cycles can be silently
    dropped because there is no kernel-visible thread to deliver it to.
    Telix avoids this by replacing signal-based wakeup with persistent
    memory-mapped completion rings: completed operations are enqueued
    atomically into a ring buffer the executor drains each poll cycle.
    A wakeup is never ``delivered'' and therefore never lost — it is
    ``enqueued'' and consumed when the executor next polls.

  \item[\textbf{Mode~3 — compute-bound monopoly.}]
    A user-level scheduler cannot preempt a compute-bound thread that
    never yields, because it lacks the kernel privilege to take a CPU
    away.  Telix addresses this with the two stack carriers of
    \S\ref{sec:preemption}: Carrier~A (a kernel stack at
    syscall/fault entry) makes kernel-side execution
    timer-preemptible, and Carrier~B (demotion to a preemptible
    carrier thread with a real stack) provides preemption for
    userspace-side compute.  Both carriers are transient; the future
    remains stackless in the common non-compute-bound case.  This
    design follows the Linux pattern of demoting overrunning softirqs
    to \texttt{ksoftirqd} kthreads: the non-preemptible fast path is
    preserved, and a bounded escape hatch provides preemption for the
    tail that needs it.
\end{description}

Telix's contribution with respect to scheduler activations is not a new
solution to the Anderson problem; it is the observation that three
well-understood, published mechanisms, when combined, avoid all three
failure modes without requiring kernel/user scheduler coordination
(upcalls).  More fundamentally, Telix takes the position that the M:N
model was not wrong — it was premature.  Anderson et al.
(1991) identified the right goal (lightweight user-level concurrency
with kernel cooperation) but lacked the mechanisms that make it
implementable without the fragile upcall machinery that sank
first-generation M:N kernels.  Io\_uring-style submission rings were
not described until Soares \& Stumm's FlexSC (2010)~\footcite{soar10}
and later popularised by Axboe's io\_uring (2019)~\footcite{axbo19};
memory-mapped completion rings are
infeasible without wide cache-coherent memory hierarchies; stackless
futures require a language-level async abstraction that did not exist
in C.  The abandonment of M:N in kernels was historically correct given
the tools of the time; Telix argues that the tools have caught up and
the original M:N insight can finally be realised.

\section{Preemption via Stack Carriers}
\label{sec:preemption}

A stackless future has no \emph{timer-preemptible} suspension point
between \texttt{await}s.  A trap (syscall or page fault) is a genuine
involuntary suspension point, but it is carried by a transient trap
frame rather than a stack, so it does not make the future stackful.
To provide real preemption of user work (so that a compute-bound
future cannot monopolise a core), Telix attaches a \emph{stack
carrier} to a future at precisely the moments where preemption
matters.  The future itself remains stackless and serialisable; the
carrier is transient and reusable.

\subsection{Carrier A: Kernel Stacks at Syscall Entry}

Each executor maintains a pool of $N$ kernel stacks (one per core; only
\emph{runnable} futures consume one).  When a user future crosses into
the kernel — via a trap, page fault, or syscall ring submission — the
entry path switches to a kernel stack, exactly as in a conventional
kernel-thread syscall entry.  From that point the kernel-side execution
is a normal preemptible context: a timer interrupt can save the kernel
frame (which contains the in-flight poll), run the EEVDF scheduler, and
switch to another future's kernel frame.  Idle and waiting futures
consume no stack, preserving the memory advantage of the M:N model.

This carrier makes \emph{kernel-side} execution preemptible — the
classic fairness hazard (kernel work monopolising a core).

\subsection{Carrier B: Demotion to a Preemptible Carrier Thread}

The remaining gap is \emph{userspace-side} compute: a future spinning in
userspace without syscalls or \texttt{await}s.  The EEVDF executor
measures elapsed wall time per poll.  If a poll exceeds a budget (a
configurable threshold, e.g., a few milliseconds), the future is
\emph{demoted}: it is migrated to a preemptible carrier thread with its
own real stack, which re-polls the future.  A timer interrupt can now
preempt that thread anywhere, including mid-poll in userspace.  When the
future later blocks (hits an \texttt{await}) or its fair share is
restored, it may be promoted back to the cooperative executor.

This mirrors the Linux pattern of demoting overrunning softirqs to
\texttt{ksoftirqd} kthreads: the non-preemptible fast path is preserved,
and a bounded escape hatch provides preemption for the tail that needs
it.

