\section{Hierarchical Fair-Share Allocation}
\label{sec:fair_share}

To support multi-tenant isolation, containerization, and privilege
domain separation, Telix organizes execution entities into a
\textbf{Hierarchical Scheduling Tree}. Resource weights are distributed
proportionally from root system nodes down to individual execution
tasks.

\subsection{Proportional Weight Distribution}

Let each node $k$ in the scheduling hierarchy be assigned a relative
weight $w_k > 0$. The share of physical CPU bandwidth $S_i$ allocated to
a leaf task $i$ descending from a tree path $(r, \dots, p, i)$ is
calculated as the product of proportional shares along its branch:

\begin{equation}
    S_i = \prod_{k \in \textrm{Ancestors}(i)} \frac{w_k}{\sum_{j \in \textrm{Siblings}(k)} w_j}
\end{equation}

This tree structure ensures that:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Domain Isolation:} A high-load tenant domain cannot starve neighboring domains, as the total aggregate consumption of the subtree is capped by its branch weight.
    \item \textbf{Dynamic Group Rebalancing:} Adding or destroying tasks within a leaf group automatically redistributes that group's allocated share internal to its node without perturbing global schedule invariants.
\end{itemize}

\section{Earliest Eligible Virtual Deadline First (EEVDF) Engine}
\label{sec:eevdf_engine}

At the core node level, Telix implements EEVDF to enforce both
fair-share proportional service and strict latency bounds. Unlike
completely fair schedulers (CFS) that optimize solely for accumulated
virtual runtime ($v_i$), EEVDF explicitly calculates both
\textbf{eligibility} (fairness) and \textbf{virtual deadlines} (latency
sensitivity).

\subsection{Virtual Time and Lag Accounting}

EEVDF is instantiated \emph{per executor} (one per physical CPU core).
Each executor~$e$ maintains its own virtual time $V_e(t)$, active weight
$W_e(t)$, and eligible-future red-black tree.  This avoids the
cache-coherence tax of a single global runqueue and maps naturally to the
per-core interrupt delivery model of the TCB layer.

System progression for executor $e$ is governed by its local virtual time
$V_e(t)$, which tracks the normalized service received by all active user
futures assigned to that executor. The total active weight of executor
$e$ at physical time $t$ is:
\begin{equation}
    W(t) = \sum_{j \in \textrm{Active}} w_j
\end{equation}

Local virtual time for executor $e$ advances according to physical
execution duration $\Delta t$ spent executing user futures on that core:
\begin{equation}
    V_e(t + \Delta t) = V_e(t) + \frac{\Delta t}{W_e(t)}
\end{equation}

(TCB-internal continuations do not contribute to virtual time: they are
overhead charged to \emph{all} futures proportionally, as in the Linux
softirq model.)

Each user future tracks its individual virtual execution time $v_i(t)$.
The difference between the fluid-model target service and actual service
rendered defines the future's \textbf{lag} $L_i(t)$:
\begin{equation}
    L_i(t) = w_i \cdot \big( V_e(t) - v_i(t) \big)
\end{equation}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Positive Lag ($L_i > 0$):} Future $i$ has received less service than its allocated fair share (the executor owes execution time to the future).
    \item \textbf{Negative Lag ($L_i < 0$):} Future $i$ has consumed more than its fair share (the future must yield priority to peers).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Eligibility and Virtual Deadline Calculation}

To prevent latency-sensitive tasks with small requested execution
quantums ($q_i$) from being starved by compute-bound workloads, EEVDF
divides dispatch evaluation into two phases:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Eligibility Criterion:} A future $i$ on executor $e$ is eligible to be polled at virtual time $V_e(t)$ if and only if its virtual time does not exceed $V_e(t)$:
    \begin{equation}
        V_e(t) \ge v_i(t) \quad \iff \quad L_i(t) \ge 0
    \end{equation}
    Ineligible futures ($L_i < 0$) are moved to a hold queue until $V_e(t)$ advances to match $v_i(t)$.

    \item \textbf{Virtual Deadline Selection:} Among all *eligible* futures, the executor selects the future with the minimum virtual deadline $d_i$. Given a requested slice quantum $q_i$, $d_i$ is computed as:
    \begin{equation}
        d_i = v_i(t) + \frac{q_i}{w_i}
    \end{equation}
\end{enumerate}

By requesting a small time slice $q_i$, latency-critical user futures
(such as network-stack polling or interactive GUI render loops) achieve
earlier virtual deadlines, obtaining prompt re-poll without exceeding
their long-term weighted fair-share allocation.  TCB-internal continuations
do not participate in this scheduling loop at all: they run immediately
on interrupt delivery, preempting whichever user future is currently
executing on that core.

\subsection{Async Continuation Polling Integration}

The cooperative fast path proceeds as follows.  In Telix's Framekernel
domains, ready user futures are indexed in an augmented Red-Black tree
per executor, sorted by $d_i$. Execution consists of invoking
\texttt{Future::poll()}:

\begin{itemize}
    \item If the continuation returns \texttt{Poll::Ready}, execution completes, the task is evicted from the tree, and its remaining positive or negative lag balance $L_i$ is amortized across active domain peers.
    \item If the continuation returns \texttt{Poll::Pending}, the elapsed physical CPU time $\Delta t$ is charged to $v_i$, $d_i$ is updated based on the remaining quantum slice, and the executor proceeds to poll the next eligible continuation.
\end{itemize}

\section{Partitioned and Gang Coscheduling}
\label{sec:coscheduling}

While EEVDF provides optimal single-core thread fairness,
inter-partition workloads (such as distributed capability channels or
parallel workloads across multikernel nodes) suffer severe latency
degradation if communicating processes are not scheduled simultaneously.

\subsection{Cross-Partition IPC Bottlenecks}

If Partition 0 transmits an asynchronous message over a ring buffer to
Partition 1, but Partition 1's local core is executing an unrelated
background task, the message sits unconsumed in memory, resulting in
severe cascade blocking.

